                  %a{:href => "#StrongSymmetricMonoidalEquialence"} Strong symmetric monoidal equivalence
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#EquivalenceOfSuperLieAlgebraCohomology"} Equivalence of super Lie algebra cohomology in both conventions
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#internalization_sign_rule"} Internalization sign rule
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#super_odd_sign_rule"} Super odd sign rule
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superalgebra"} superalgebra
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            involves, by definition, crucial minus signs appearing in certain expressions. While there is a systematic rule for these, when superalgebra/supergeometry is combined with other topics that crucially involve their own signs, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+algebra"} noncommutative algebra
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#DeligneFreed99"} Deligne-Freed 99
          %p
            The main point to notice is that for algebraic terms that have both a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{Z}
            and a super-degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \in \mathbb{Z}_2
            the “internalization sign rule” for the sign when exchanging two such elements is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi σ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo )
                    %mo ,
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi σ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo )
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ↦
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 2
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi σ
                        %mn 2
                      %msub
                        %mi σ
                        %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\left(
                  \\left(
                  n_1, \sigma_1
                  \\right)
                  ,
                  \\left(
                  n_2, \sigma_2
                  \\right)
                  \\right)
                  \\;\mapsto\;
                  (-1)^{n_1 n_2 + \sigma_2 \sigma_2}
                  \\,.
          %p
            (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#CastellaniDAuriaFre91"} Castellani-D’Auria-Fré 91, (II.2.109)
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#BonoraBregolaLechnerPastiTonin87"} Bonora-Bregola-Lechner-Pasti-Tonin 87, beginning of section 2
          %p
            This we discuss below at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#TheSignRuleFromInternalization"} The sign rule from internalization
          %p Parts of the literature however adops another sign rule, given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi σ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo )
                    %mo ,
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi σ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo )
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ↦
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi σ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 2
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi σ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\left(
                  \\left(
                  n_1, \sigma_1
                  \\right)
                  ,
                  \\left(
                  n_2, \sigma_2
                  \\right)
                  \\right)
                  \\;\mapsto\;
                  (-1)^{(n_1 + \sigma_1)(n_2 + \sigma_2)}
                  \\,.
          %p
            This we discuss below in
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#SuperOddConvention"} The super odd sign rule
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#DeligneMorgan99"} Deligne-Morgan 99
            %em Deligne’s convention
            (for the internalization rule) and
            %em Bernstein’s convention
            (for the super odd sign rule).
